# About This Book
#
# Canonical Litex module: scripts/high_school_book/textbook
# Published module: textbooks/High-School-Mathematics
#
# This book is a compact, runnable map of high-school mathematics expressed in
# Litex. Its source of truth is the 20-unit curriculum inventory maintained in
# `scripts/high_school_book/knowledge_core_json/`, rather than the prose of one
# external textbook. The chapters introduce reusable definitions, state the
# main relationships between them, and keep representative calculations close
# to the concepts they illustrate.
#
# Mathematical directions
#
# 1. Sets and logic (Chapter 1). Membership, subsets, finite sets, set
#    operations, implication, case analysis, and contradiction form the common
#    language used by the rest of the book.
#
# 2. Algebra (Chapters 2--3). Equations, quadratic roots, inequalities, means,
#    powers, exponentials, and logarithms develop symbolic calculation over the
#    standard numeric carriers.
#
# 3. Functions (Chapter 4). Real functions are studied through values, graphs,
#    ranges, parity, monotonicity, zeros, extrema, continuity, and inverse
#    behavior.
#
# 4. Trigonometry (Chapters 5--6). Degree and radian measure, sine, cosine,
#    tangent, identities, periods, amplitudes, and the sine and cosine laws
#    connect algebraic formulas with angle and triangle calculations.
#
# 5. Plane vectors and analytic geometry (Chapters 7 and 13--15). Coordinate
#    vector operations, dot products, lines, circles, conics, parametric and
#    polar descriptions, and space vectors turn geometric objects into usable
#    functions, sets, and relations.
#
# 6. Complex numbers (Chapter 8). Native complex arithmetic, real and
#    imaginary coordinates, conjugation, and modulus extend scalar calculation
#    beyond the real line.
#
# 7. Solid geometry and measurement (Chapters 9--10). Coordinate incidence,
#    lines and planes in space, perpendicular and parallel directions, surface
#    area, and volume formulas cover the three-dimensional geometric layer.
#
# 8. Probability (Chapters 11 and 19). Finite sample spaces, events, uniform
#    probability, independence, conditional probability, Bayes' formula, and
#    elementary distributions describe uncertainty through explicit numeric
#    models.
#
# 9. Statistics and data analysis (Chapters 12 and 20). Mean, variance,
#    standard deviation, range, relative frequency, percentile rank,
#    covariance, correlation, regression, and independence tests summarize and
#    compare finite data.
#
# 10. Sequences, induction, and combinatorics (Chapters 16 and 18). Arithmetic
#     and geometric sequences, recursive definitions, finite sums, induction,
#     permutations, combinations, Pascal's identity, and the binomial theorem
#     provide the book's discrete-mathematics direction.
#
# 11. Derivatives and elementary calculus (Chapter 17). Difference quotients,
#     derivative relations, stationary points, monotonicity, and optimization
#     show how rates of change connect functions with local behavior.
#
# How to read the module
#
# The chapters are ordered so that later files can reuse earlier carriers and
# constructions. Litex builtins supply standard numbers, sets, functions,
# finite objects, trigonometric functions, and complex scalars; the chapter
# files define the source-facing concepts that readers apply. Each direction
# includes concrete examples, not only declarations, so its main interface can
# be seen in use.
#
# After Chapter 20, eleven `*_exercises.lit` companions revisit the directions
# in this map. They contain original curriculum-based problems and short
# solutions that cite the chapter namespaces instead of redefining the book's
# concepts.
#
# Verification and scope
#
# Current release file gates pass the shared cite layer, all 20 chapters, and
# all eleven exercise companions. The chapter and exercise files contain no
# direct `trust`; five localized trusted conclusions and one abstract volume
# interface remain visible in `HighSchoolCite`. Those boundaries record
# unfinished supporting mathematics and are not claimed as verifier-checked
# proofs. The project is a runnable curriculum map with representative
# exercises, not a claim to contain every theorem or proof in high-school
# mathematics.
